### What is the difference between Comparable and Comparator?

### What is Comparable?

Comparable is implemented by the class that wants a default sort order. The class implements `Comparable<T>` and overrides `compareTo(T other)`.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class Trade implements Comparable<Trade> {
    private String tradeId;
    private BigDecimal amount;

    @Override
    public int compareTo(Trade other) {
        return this.tradeId.compareTo(other.tradeId);  // natural order = by tradeId
    }
}
```

```java
// Natural order used by:
Collections.sort(trades);                    // uses compareTo
trades.stream().sorted().toList();           // uses compareTo
new TreeSet<>(trades);                       // natural order
```

### What is Comparator?

Comparator is defined outside the class. No modification to Trade is needed.

```java
// External Comparators:
Comparator<Trade> byAmount = Comparator.comparing(Trade::getAmount);
Comparator<Trade> byAmountDesc = Comparator.comparing(Trade::getAmount).reversed();
Comparator<Trade> bySymbolThenAmount = Comparator.comparing(Trade::getSymbol)
    .thenComparing(Trade::getAmount);

// Usage:
trades.sort(byAmount);
trades.stream().sorted(byAmountDesc).toList();
new TreeSet<>(bySymbolThenAmount);
```

### How do naturalOrder and reverseOrder bridge the two?

```java
// naturalOrder: wraps Comparable.compareTo as a Comparator
Comparator<String> natural = Comparator.naturalOrder();
// Equivalent to: (a, b) -> a.compareTo(b)

// Used in nullsLast (which requires a Comparator argument)
Comparator<Trade> deptSort = Comparator.comparing(Trade::getDepartment,
    Comparator.nullsLast(Comparator.naturalOrder()));

// reverseOrder: reverse of natural ordering
Comparator<String> reversed = Comparator.reverseOrder();
trades.stream()
    .sorted(Comparator.comparing(Trade::getAmount, Comparator.reverseOrder()))
    .toList();
```

### When do you use Comparable vs Comparator?

| Aspect | Comparable | Comparator |
|---|---|---|
| Defined | Inside the class | Outside the class |
| How many | One per class | Unlimited per class |
| Interface | `compareTo(T other)` | `compare(T a, T b)` |
| Modification | Requires changing the class | No class change needed |
| Default for | sorted(), TreeSet, TreeMap | Passed as parameter |
| Example | String alphabetical order | Sort strings by length |

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: trying to sort a class that does not implement Comparable
class Account { String id; BigDecimal balance; }
List<Account> accounts = List.of(new Account(), new Account());
Collections.sort(accounts);  // ClassCastException: Account cannot be cast to Comparable

// CORRECT: provide a Comparator
accounts.sort(Comparator.comparing(Account::getBalance));
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Comparable should be consistent with equals: if `a.compareTo(b) == 0`, then `a.equals(b)` should be true. TreeSet uses compareTo for uniqueness, not equals.
- Comparator.comparing is the modern way to create comparators; avoid writing `compare()` by hand.
- The Comparator fluent API (comparing, thenComparing, reversed) follows the Strategy Pattern.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Can a class implement Comparable and still be sorted with a Comparator? (Yes. The Comparator overrides the natural order.)
- What happens if compareTo is inconsistent with equals? (TreeSet may behave differently from HashSet for the same elements.)
- Is Comparable required for TreeSet? (Either the elements must be Comparable, or you must provide a Comparator to the TreeSet constructor.)
